 2001 or a  pre-2000 or post 2001 just for our  international viewers in terms of the  way that isil is made up i mean you know  we mustn't perhaps think of it as a  legitimate force an army that is  controlled by central command  many  experts i've spoken to in the last  couple of days have talked about isil  being very isolated right now and it  could be just the odd um suicide bomber  here or there is that the sort of isil  we're looking at  it sort of is but the odd suicide bomber  here and there can do an awful lot of  damage  you know particularly against soft  targets it appears to have been them  that attacked this  shia hazara school in kabul  earlier in the year and killed 85 school  children uh there was an attack on a  hospital you know these things aren't  difficult to do  with a couple of suicide bombers uh who  don't have any expertise other than to  blow themselves up i mean it's been  quiet so far but the taliban did release  many isil operatives inadvertently in  those prison uh prison releases um do  you think the taliban are going to have  to deal with isil in some shape or form  sooner rather than later  i think they will after one they've won  a great victory and they'll want to  assert their power over all of  afghanistan  they'll also want uh acceptance by uh  other countries you know for reasons  we've just been hearing about now they  need aid they need food and so forth  so it's in their interest to say we are  the barrier against isis you may not  like us but there's a that could be  worse to come so it's much in their  interest to do that at the same time  they  the taliban and isil and al-qaeda do  appeal to the same constituency at  various uh points they're  interchangeable some of the isil many of  the isil leaders are full and former  taliban so you two have these two  different processes uh an impulse for  the taliban to crack down which is a  very strong but at the same time isil  isn't so far different  uh from uh their own organization  something else really to ponder on  because  they've been sworn enemies so they say  for many years fighting you might say a  puppet government in kabul supported by  the west so how does the current  situation then change the way isil if  they are well organized and funded from  acting successfully in a taliban  controlled afghanistan you know will  they see the taliban now as much as the  enemy  as the west because they have a  different sort of islamic ideology  well i saw you know as we saw in iraq  and syria tends to think of practically  everybody as an enemy apart from itself  and he's prepared perfectly prepared to  kill them and we have countless examples  of that so this might well be so i mean  they've already said this isn't uh the  taliban victory isn't uh  as pure as to the taliban claim so i  think they will uh contest it  um  but uh there's also a good reason why  the taliban should try to suppress them  or at least present themselves as the  barrier against isil  and use that as leverage with foreign  states in order to get recognition to  get aid and so forth we should see what  happens in the coming days for the  moment patrick coben thanks so much for  joining us from cork in ireland thank  you
 

